# Tests and regression coverage

## New features

- **Ship tests with the feature** in [`Tests/TheDungeon.Tests/`](Tests/TheDungeon.Tests) (xUnit). Coverage should be **meaningful**: exercise the new behavior, important branches, and invariants—not only happy path.
- **Prefer testable seams:** keep rules/resolve/mapping/presenter decision logic in tiered C# (`Scripts/**`) with unit tests. Godot scene scripts under `Scenes/**` that contain non-trivial behavior should be covered where practical (pure helpers extracted if needed).
- **End of work:** run **`dotnet test`** (or the project’s test command) and fix failures before considering the feature done.

## Bugs and edge-case tweaks

- **Every bug fix or edge-case change** that touches behavior should include a **regression test** (new test or extended test) that would have failed before the fix or that locks the intended edge case.
- If the issue was “no behavior change, only editor wiring,” document that in the PR/commit instead of a fake test; still add a test when any **C#** behavior changed.

## What “robust” means here

- At least: **one focused test per public behavior** added or fixed; for resolvers/mappers, cover **null/empty/no-match** paths where relevant.
- Avoid huge integration tests unless necessary; **small, fast unit tests** over duplicate suites.

## Exceptions (use sparingly)

- **Serialized Godot assets** (`*.tscn`, `*.tres`)-only changes—no C# test (editor-owned; see the Godot serialized resources rule in this repo); manual or play-test note instead.
- Truly **cosmetic** UI with zero logic change—no test required.

When in doubt, **add the test.**
